diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 4479b53..bd7a6b3 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -15,3 +15,32 @@ The application will now be running on `http://localhost:11224`.
 - **[React](https://react.dev/)**: Used for UI components.
 - **[Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com/)**: For styling the application.
 - **[Astro DB](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/database/)**: Powers the comments feature, using a libSQL-compatible database.
+
+## Browser tools (WebMCP)
+
+The shared layout registers client-side tools with `document.modelContext`, with
+support for the earlier `navigator.modelContext` API. Browsers without WebMCP
+continue to use the normal buttons and forms. Tools stay registered across Astro
+client navigation and read current page/session state when invoked.
+
+- `search_articles({ query })`: search titles and summaries and open the search flyout.
+- `get_reading_list({})`: return saved articles and open the reading-list flyout.
+- `toggle_reading_list({ slug })`: add an unsaved article or remove a saved article;
+  updates the open list and bookmark buttons using the existing session API.
+- `get_current_article({})`: return the current article title, body text and URL.
+- `get_article_popularity({ id })`, `get_coffee_brew({})`, `get_plant_stats({})`,
+  `get_tide_levels({})`: call the article widget APIs. The intentional demo endpoint
+  failures are preserved and returned as `{ isError: true, error }`.
+
+Opening either flyout also exposes a declarative form tool,
+`search_articles_form({ query })` or `reading_list_form({ slug })`. These forms
+include named, described fields and return structured results through
+`SubmitEvent.respondWith` for agent submissions. The reading-list form toggles
+membership, just like the bookmark button. Ordinary submissions work without
+WebMCP. The implementation follows Chrome's
+[imperative API](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/webmcp/imperative-api) and
+[declarative API](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/webmcp/declarative-api).
+
+Run `pnpm test` for client-only tests using mocked browser APIs and fetch;
+`pnpm run build` checks the production bundles. Neither command starts a server.
+Tests require Node 22.13 or newer for built-in TypeScript stripping.
